// The Spring Boot vocabulary: what a service's own facts are called once they are in the graph.
//
// This file is a `.nost` document and nothing else, so the Engine validates it — `nostdb check` reads it the
// same way it reads any other. This Skill holds no validator of its own, and a malformed preset fails before
// it is ever offered to a model.
//
// # What it is for
//
// `nostdb-spec/docs/NOST_LANGUAGE.md` accepts a consequence rather than solving it: a record may name a
// schema that was never declared, so a misspelled label is indistinguishable from an intentional bare one.
// A preset fixes the vocabulary on the producing side, which is the only side where the two can be told
// apart. Without one, a model proposing `Job` today and `ScheduledTask` tomorrow produces two unvalidated
// labels and no error, and the graph holds both readings for ever.
//
// # What it is not
//
// It is not an analyzer. Nothing here reads `@Scheduled`; these are the names a *proposal* must use once
// something else has read it, and the Engine is what checks the proposal against them.
//
// # What it does not cover
//
// Persistence **mappings** — `@Entity`, `@Column`, and the repository interfaces over them — belong to the
// `jpa` preset the `nostdb` Skill ships. Two presets declaring one label would leave whichever was applied
// last standing, and the loss would show only as a warning.
//
// The division is by source rather than by subject. `jpa` describes what a class says it maps to; this file
// describes the store that is actually there, which comes from DDL, migrations, and configuration. Where the
// two meet, they meet on a name: `Entity.table` and `Table.name` hold the same string, and a query joins them
// on it. No edge is declared between the two vocabularies, because an edge into another preset's labels would
// warn on every project that installed only one of them.
//
// # Three names this file may not use
//
// - **a label a build already writes.** `Endpoint`, `File`, `Directory`, `Module`, `Method`, `Field`, and the
//   rest are declared by every build. A schema is unowned, so a preset redeclaring one is replaced on the
//   next build — the preset's version vanishes and the only sign is a warning. So the route record is not
//   redeclared here: `Endpoint` is *referenced* in an endpoint constraint, and this file's records hang off
//   it. Settings hang off `File` for the same reason, rather than off a `ConfigFile` that would put a second
//   record on a path the scan already recorded;
// - **a label the `jpa` preset declares**, which is why a physical column is `TableColumn`;
// - **a builtin relation name.** `HANDLED_BY` runs from `Endpoint` to `Method` in every build, so a schema
//   for it here would raise a violation on the Engine's own edges. A job reaches its declaration through
//   `RUNS`.
//
// # One value shape a proposal cannot carry yet
//
// Five fields here are lists: `Request.path_variables`, `query_parameters`, and `headers`, `Table.primary_key`,
// and `Collection.indexes`. The `.nost` language has list properties, storage holds them, and a schema may
// declare `string[]` — `nostdb check` accepts this file — but a **change set** cannot carry one. Its property
// reader takes a boolean, a string, and a number, and refuses an array with `unsupported property value`.
//
// So those five are settable by hand-writing `.nost` and not by the route a proposal takes. They are declared
// as lists anyway, because a composite primary key *is* a list and flattening it into a comma-joined string
// would put a format in the graph that every reader then has to parse. `SKILL.md` says to leave them out of a
// proposal, which is the same treatment the relations below get and for the same reason.
//
// `Schema` is deliberately absent as a name. NostDB already has schemas — this file is made of them — and a
// label called `Schema` would make `MATCH (s:Schema)` mean two things at once.

@nost 4

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The web contract
//
// A build already records the route: `Endpoint` carries its method, path, and handler, drawn by the framework
// analyzer from the mapping annotations. What no analyzer reads is the shape on either side of it, which is
// what these three describe.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// What a route accepts.
//
// One record per distinct payload, named so two routes sharing a body type share one reading of it.
//
// Every field but the name is optional because a route may accept nothing at all — a bare `GET /health` takes
// no body, no variables, and no parameters — and because absent means "not written down" rather than "none".
// `media_type` is absent unless the source states one: Spring supplies a default, and recording that default
// here would be reporting a decision the source did not make.
schema Request {
  name: string,
  media_type?: string,
  body_type?: string,
  path_variables?: string[],
  query_parameters?: string[],
  headers?: string[]
}

// What a route returns.
//
// A route may declare several — a success and a failure shape — so this is a record rather than fields on the
// endpoint, and `name` is what tells them apart.
//
// `status` is absent unless the source states one. Spring answers 200 when nothing said otherwise, and
// writing 200 into a record no annotation asked for would put a claim in the graph that nobody made.
schema Response {
  name: string,
  status?: integer,
  media_type?: string,
  body_type?: string
}

// A constraint the source states, wherever it states it.
//
// One record with a `kind` rather than a label per kind, for the reason the `jpa` preset gives about
// cardinality: a query asking what constrains a route should not have to name every kind of constraint to
// avoid missing one. `kind` is a free string — `validation`, `database`, `authorization`, `business` — and not
// a closed list, because a project's own constraint vocabulary is not this file's to bound.
//
// `expression` holds the constraint **as written**, unevaluated: `min=1,max=64`, a SQL `CHECK` body, or a
// property placeholder. Evaluating one would mean resolving configuration this graph has not read, which is
// the same reason a framework analyzer records `${api.base}` rather than substituting it.
//
// `applies_to` names the field or column the rule is about. It earns its place because a rule usually applies
// to one member of a record that has no record of its own: a `@Size` on one field of a request body, where
// the body is a single `Request`. The edge says which record is constrained; this says which part of it.
schema Rule {
  name: string,
  kind: string,
  expression?: string,
  message?: string,
  applies_to?: string
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The data store
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// A configured connection to a data store.
//
// **There is deliberately no `url` field.** A JDBC or R2DBC URL routinely carries a user and a password, and
// a preset with a field for one invites recording it — the root contract forbids a credential reaching a
// graph file, and a field that cannot hold one enforces that better than a rule asking nobody to. The parts
// that matter for a graph are here separately, and none of them can carry a secret.
//
// `kind` is a free string — `jdbc`, `r2dbc`, `mongo`, `redis`, `elasticsearch` — because storage here is
// meant to be neutral about which stores exist, the same way the Engine is neutral about which languages do.
schema Datasource {
  name: string,
  kind: string,
  driver?: string,
  host?: string,
  port?: integer,
  database?: string,
  schema_name?: string
}

// A relational table.
//
// The table that is actually there, read from DDL or a migration rather than from a mapping annotation. What
// a class says it maps to is `Entity.table` in the `jpa` preset, and the two meet on this record's `name`.
//
// `primary_key` is a list because a composite key is one, and absent when the source did not say — a table
// with no stated key is a fact worth keeping as absent rather than as an empty list, which would claim the
// source said "none".
schema Table {
  name: string,
  schema_name?: string,
  primary_key?: string[]
}

// One column of a table.
//
// `TableColumn` rather than `Column`, which the `jpa` preset declares for an ORM field mapping. The two are
// different facts from different sources — one is what the store has, the other is what a class says it binds
// to — and one name for both would lose whichever preset was applied first.
schema TableColumn {
  name: string,
  data_type?: string,
  nullable?: boolean,
  default_value?: string
}

// A document collection.
//
// Separate from `Table` rather than a `Table` with a `kind`, because the two carry different facts: a
// collection has indexes and no column list, and a query over one is not a query over the other.
schema Collection {
  name: string,
  database?: string,
  indexes?: string[]
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Work the framework invokes
//
// A route is reached from outside the program. These are reached by the framework itself, which is why they
// are records of their own: nothing in the call graph points at them, so a reader looking only at `CALLS`
// would conclude they are dead.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// A unit of work nothing calls.
//
// `kind` says how the framework reaches it — `scheduled`, `async`, `listener`, `startup`, `shutdown` — and
// `source` names the topic, queue, or event it reads when it is a listener, absent when there is nothing to
// name.
//
// `handler` is the declaration's name as written, and the `RUNS` edge is the resolved link to it. Both,
// because that is what a build already does for a route: `Endpoint` carries a `handler` string and draws
// `HANDLED_BY` beside it, so a name that resolved to nothing is still recorded rather than lost.
schema Job {
  name: string,
  kind: string,
  source?: string,
  handler?: string
}

// When a scheduled job runs.
//
// A record rather than fields on `Job`, because `@Scheduled` is repeatable: one method can carry several
// triggers, and fields would hold only the last one read.
//
// `expression` is as written, which is often a property placeholder rather than a cron string.
// `initial_delay` is a string for the same reason — it may be a placeholder or a duration, and parsing it
// into a number would discard the spelling the source chose.
schema Schedule {
  kind: string,
  expression: string,
  zone?: string,
  initial_delay?: string
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Configuration and build
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// One configured key.
//
// Hung off the builtin `File` record for the document that declares it — see `DECLARES_SETTING`. A
// `ConfigFile` of its own would put a second record on a path the scan already recorded, and "which file is
// application.yaml" would return two.
//
// **`secret` means the value is not here.** A settings file holds tokens and passwords beside ports and
// timeouts, and the root contract forbids either reaching a graph file. So the flag does not classify the
// setting as sensitive and leave the value beside it: it says the value was deliberately withheld, which is
// what lets a reader tell that from "the source did not set one".
//
// `profile` is the Spring profile the value belongs to, absent for the default document.
schema Setting {
  key: string,
  value?: string,
  secret?: boolean,
  profile?: string
}

// A declared library dependency.
//
// `version` is absent when the build file does not state one — a platform, a BOM, or a version catalog
// supplies it — and absent says exactly that rather than guessing what the build would resolve.
//
// `catalog_key` holds the alias when the version came from a catalog, as written: `libs.spring.boot.web`.
// Recording the alias rather than resolving it keeps this a fact about the build file, and the catalog's own
// entries are `Setting` records under the `File` that declares them.
schema Dependency {
  artifact: string,
  group?: string,
  version?: string,
  scope?: string,
  catalog_key?: string
}

// One module of a build.
//
// `BuildModule` rather than `Module`, which a build already writes for a module of the analyzed programming
// language. These are different things: one is a Kotlin or Java namespace, the other is a unit Gradle or
// Maven builds.
//
// This is what answers whether a tree holds one project or several. A repository with three of these is a
// multi-module build, and `INCLUDES_MODULE` says which contains which.
schema BuildModule {
  name: string,
  kind: string,
  path?: string,
  packaging?: string
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The relations
//
// Where a relation runs between one pair of shapes it is constrained, so the Engine refuses an edge between
// the wrong two. Where it genuinely runs between several it is declared without endpoints, which is what
// `nostdb-core` does for `CONTAINS` and for the same reason: one constraint cannot describe five shapes, and
// declaring one would raise a violation on every edge of the others.
//
// # Five of these reach a record a build wrote, and that needs its identifier
//
// A change set names an edge's endpoint by opaque identifier — `{"local": "n_…"}` — so an edge to `Endpoint`,
// `File`, `Method`, or `Directory` needs the identifier the Engine minted for it. One query gives it:
//
//   nostdb query "MATCH (e:Endpoint) RETURN e AS record, e.path AS path" --project . --format json
//   -> [{"node": "n_019fb6a0-43b7-7942-8c96-a7c7fcf8341c"}, "/users"]
//
// `nostdb export --nost` writes the same identifier as each record's reserved `id` property, which is the
// route to use when a whole graph is being read rather than one pattern.
//
// This replaces a note that said the opposite. It was written after looking for a `nostdb.id()` function and
// for an `id` property in a table-formatted result — neither exists — and concluding there was no route
// without returning the record itself. Five relations were marked unproposable and one of them was then
// proposed, applied, and queried across the boundary.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// A route to what it accepts. `Endpoint` is the record a build already writes.
//
// Reaches a record a build wrote, so its identifier comes from a query — see the header.
schema ACCEPTS(Endpoint -> Request) {
}

// A route to what it returns.
//
// Reaches a record a build wrote, so its identifier comes from a query — see the header.
schema RETURNS(Endpoint -> Response) {
}

// Anything constrained, to the rule constraining it.
//
// No endpoint constraint: a rule can constrain a route, a request payload, a table, or one of its columns,
// and one pair of shapes cannot say that. Declared anyway rather than left out, because the point of a preset
// is fixing the name — a proposal writing `VALIDATED_BY` would produce a second relation type for one fact.
//
// Most rules sit between this file's own records — a rule over a request payload or a column — and one from
// `Endpoint` needs that record's identifier like the two above.
schema CONSTRAINED_BY {
}

// A route or a job, to the table or collection it reads or writes.
//
// No endpoint constraint, for the reason above: four shapes reach two.
//
// `mode` rather than `READS` and `WRITES` as separate relations, following the `jpa` preset's reasoning about
// cardinality: a query asking what touches a table should not have to name every mode to avoid missing one.
// `read`, `write`, or `read_write`.
//
// From `Job`, which this file owns, or from `Endpoint` with that record's identifier.
schema ACCESSES {
  mode: string
}

// A job to a trigger that fires it. Several, when the source declares several.
schema TRIGGERED_BY(Job -> Schedule) {
}

// A job to the declaration the framework invokes.
//
// `RUNS` rather than `HANDLED_BY`, which a build already uses from `Endpoint` to `Method`. Declaring a schema
// for a builtin relation would constrain the Engine's own edges to this file's shape and warn on every one of
// them.
//
// Reaches a record a build wrote, so its identifier comes from a query. `Job.handler` still carries the name,
// because a handler that resolved to nothing is a fact worth keeping and an edge cannot hold it.
schema RUNS(Job -> Method) {
}

// A data store to a table in it.
schema HAS_TABLE(Datasource -> Table) {
}

// A data store to a collection in it.
schema HAS_COLLECTION(Datasource -> Collection) {
}

// A table to one of its columns.
schema HAS_TABLE_COLUMN(Table -> TableColumn) {
}

// The document that declares a setting, to the setting.
//
// From the builtin `File`, so a settings document is the record the scan already made of it. This is what
// makes `application.yaml`, `libs.versions.toml`, `gradle.properties`, and a `build.gradle.kts` all reachable
// as themselves: each is a `File` the scan recorded, and what was read out of it hangs here.
//
// Reaches a record a build wrote, so its identifier comes from a query. `Setting.profile` still distinguishes
// two documents' readings of one key, which the edge alone would not.
schema DECLARES_SETTING(File -> Setting) {
}

// A setting to the data store it configures.
//
// Constrained to one shape deliberately, although a setting configures more than a data store. The others
// have no record here to point at — a timeout or a log level configures the framework, which this vocabulary
// does not describe — and a relation with no second shape to reach is better left narrow than widened for a
// target that does not exist.
schema CONFIGURES(Setting -> Datasource) {
}

// A build module to a library it declares.
schema DECLARES_DEPENDENCY(BuildModule -> Dependency) {
}

// A build module to a module it contains.
//
// The relation that makes a multi-module build readable as one. A root module with three of these is the
// answer to whether a tree holds one project or several.
schema INCLUDES_MODULE(BuildModule -> BuildModule) {
}

// A build module to the directory it is rooted at.
//
// To the builtin `Directory` the scan recorded, which is what lets a reader go from a file to the module that
// builds it without matching path prefixes by hand.
//
// Reaches a record a build wrote, so its identifier comes from a query. `BuildModule.path` carries the
// directory as written, which is what a build file states.
schema ROOTED_AT(BuildModule -> Directory) {
}
